{"kind":"assert","text":"Kernel ruling: step A, subject admission, is permitted under\nsections 7 and 7.1 of `docs/kernel.md`. The owner decided it on\n15 November 2026.\n\nWhy it is kernel. The admission decides whether the judge compares a\nrevision, and so whether an entry is admitted or refused. The\nruntime and the verifier must derive one answer from the pinned\ndefinition. A service that a host can replace cannot decide it.\n\nWhat the ruling covers, and nothing more:\n\n1. In `packages/contract`: the type of the part, the optional\n   member `admission` of a declared definition, and the optional\n   member `current` of an act.\n2. In `packages/derive`: the export `expectedKeys`, the validator's\n   checks of the part, the problem code `admission`, and one\n   condition in the judge.\n3. Version 1 is narrow: no platform declaration carries the part,\n   and only the recorded level admits it.\n4. No install gate. A room at any Rules pin installs a definition\n   with the part when its Worker has the code.\n\nStep A closes no K item. It adds nothing to K1's count and nothing\nto K4's list. It adds one form, `current`, to each copy of the\ngrammar that K5 names. It adds no scope kind, platform name,\ncapability name, field type, entry kind, level, reason code of a\nresult, or tool family.\n\nConditions on the heads:\n\n5. Each row of the definition corpus keeps its bytes, its digest and\n   its validation answer.\n6. The head of step A1 changes no answer of the judge and no test\n   of the judge or the verifier.\n7. The head of step A2 writes this ruling into sections 7 and 7.1\n   of the kernel page, and promise 3 gains the part.\n\nThe design is `notes/2026-11-14-subject-admission.md`. A later\nstricter check of the list takes version 2 of the part and its own\nruling.","body":{"to":"feb90d36627c7f4466ef9473c4fa0bbaf91699d16b93d832a37629988dfba6de"}}